  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\nIssued at 1248 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nThe remnants of Isaias have fully merged with an upper trough,\nbecoming extratropical last night. The resulting upper system\nwill continue northward today with a bit of a retrograde into\nWest Tennessee through this afternoon before being kicked out\neast late Sunday. Precipitation is evident on regional radars,\nbut surface observations throughout our region point to an\nunderperformance of rainfall accumulation compared to MRMS/dual-\npol estimates. The main culprit is seen on the 12z Nashville\nsounding with a deep layer of relatively dry air up to 700 mb\ncausing high amounts of evaporation. Therefore, remaining\nrainfall accumulation is expected to remain at or below 1.00\",\nmaximized along the Tennessee River and has allowed for the\ncancellation of the Flood Watch. However, the Wind Advisory does\nremain for areas east of the Mississippi River through 8 PM CDT.\nAlthough winds have struggled to gust anywhere close to 40 mph,\nCAMs still show 30 - 40 mph winds through this afternoon. It is\nlikely that evaporative cooling from precipitation may allow for\nenough sinking motion below 700 mb for the downward transfer of\nmomentum to the surface. Winds in this layer have already been\nsampled as high as 50 knots from the OHX/NQA/GWX VADs. So, there\nis still potential for these winds to bring Wind Advisory\ncriteria gusts for the next several hours.\n\nThe remnants of Isaias will still exist over the region Sunday,\nbut dry air entrainment on the northwest side of the trough will\nsteadily eat away at rain coverage through the early morning\nhours. By sunrise, the Mid-South is expected to be precipitation-\nfree with a notable decrease in winds. Sunshine will then return\nin most areas, which in conjunction with dewpoints in the low 60s\nor upper 50s will lead to highs in the upper 80s. Areas along the\nTennessee River may hang on to cloud cover for longer, which\nwould keep highs in the upper 70s. As the upper system continues\neast into early next week, a warming trend will commence with\nhighs gradually sneaking back into the low 90s as an upper ridge\ndevelops over the southern CONUS through the middle of next week\nwith dry weather expected.\n\nMedium-range guidance shows a new shortwave trough traversing the\nRockies by Wednesday. However, there is a large amount of\nvariability in the evolution of this trough with some guidance\nbringing it east through the end of the week and others allowing\nit to become cutoff over the Rockies. LREF clusters seem to\nprefer the trough to stall or only slowly propagate east, which\nwould keep the region drier and warmer through the end of next\nweek, but by Saturday they heavily favor the return of\nnorthwesterlies across the eastern CONUS. Most guidance, both\ndeterministic and ensemble, then have a front following this\npattern change into next weekend, offering the next best chance\nat cooler, more fall-like weather. All things considered though,\nthis is late in the forecast and is very much subject to changes\nin following forecasts.",
